  "query_preview": "(This evening through Sunday)...\nIssued at 340 PM EDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nThis Afternoon into Monday Night...\n\nA highly uncertain forecast scenario remains the case again today, \nparticularly with respect to the severe thunderstorm threat from \nlate today into Monday night, with multiple scenarios on the table. \nAs is often the case with these depth of the warm season setups, the \nexact evolution of each convective round will be largely \nthermodynamically driven and heavily influenced by the prior rounds, \nresulting in low confidence in the exact details as time goes on.\n\nA volatile airmass is in place this afternoon to our southwest \nacross the mid Mississippi and far lower Ohio Valleys, along and \nwest of a frontal zone, characterized by temperatures in the mid 80s \nto mid 90s and dewpoints as high as the low 80s, resulting in MLCAPE \nvalues near 5000 J/kg, along with modest to strong effective shear \nvalues of 25-40KT overlapping. This should promote thunderstorm \ndevelopment late this afternoon into this evening as a subtle upper \nlevel disturbance slides southeast along the periphery of the upper \nlevel ridge, and this activity may nudge into the southwestern \nportions of the forecast area this evening. Will stick with chance \nPoPs for now, as it is unclear how far east this activity will make \nit, as the instability gradient will likely result in storm motion \nwith a largely southerly component, and substantial convective \ninhibition remains across central Indiana on the milder side of the \ncloud-reinforced differential heating frontal boundary.\n\nAdditional severe convection is likely to develop over the upper \nMidwest, and this activity could make it into central Indiana as \nearly as the pre-dawn hours Monday, though the unfavorable diurnal \ntiming may limit the severe threat should this come to pass. With \nboth of these possible rounds of thunderstorms, damaging winds would \nbe the primary concern.\nTrimmed high temperatures back a couple/few degrees with low clouds \nand fog impacting morning insolation. Clouds are beginning to \nscatter out however, so do not expect nearly as strong an impact to \nhigh temperatures as what occurred yesterday, with the mid to upper \n80s still well in reach today, and perhaps even a 90 degree reading \nsneaking into our far southwest.\n\nPartly cloudy skies can be expected this afternoon with warm and \nhumid conditions driving heat index values into the 90s across \nportions of the area.Another round of convection appears likely \nto initiate somewhere over the upper Great Lakes tomorrow \nafternoon associated with an upper level shortwave and surface \ncold front, again likely dropping southerly along an instability \ngradient in a continued volatile airmass characterized by ample \nshear and instability, along with precipitable water values at \ntimes in the vicinity of 2 inches. This will present again a \nprimarily damaging wind threat, though an isolated tornado cannot \nbe ruled out. Additionally, the aforementioned moist airmass along\nwith deep warm rain processes as the wet bulb zero height remains\nseasonably high will present a heavy rainfall and localized flash\nflood threat, limited by the relatively progressive nature of any\nthunderstorm complexes - but a fairly strong westerly low level \njet ahead of the cold front could produce some \nregeneration/training potential on the western flank of any MCS \ndepending upon cold pool orientation.\n\nTemperatures will have the potential to soar well into the 80s or \nlow 90s on Monday afternoon, though this, too, is uncertain as any \nearly day convective debris cloudiness could limit the impact of \nwarm advection and insolation - the more sun, the greater the \npotential for highs getting to the 90 degree mark, which may couple \nwith dewpoints well into the 70s to produce heat index values at or \nabove 100 degrees Monday afternoon. \n\nTuesday onward...\n\nCold frontal passage Monday night into early Tuesday will bring \ntemperatures back down much closer to seasonal normals from mid week \nonward, with a largely dry forecast until very late in the week as \nsurface high pressure dominates the area. As the ridge aloft \nretrogrades slightly and the overall pattern amplifies, upper level \ndisturbances late in the week may promote rain chances, though \nsignificant disagreement on these systems precludes more than a \nslight chance to low chance PoP in any one period from Friday into \nthe weekend.",
